---

<BaseLayout
  title="JIS N150 vs N200 — Which Battery Do You Need?"
  description="N150 (145G51) vs N200 (190H52): the difference in size, capacity, CCA, reserve capacity and 24V pairing — with a buyer decision guide for JIS-market trucks."
  canonical="/compare/n150-vs-n200/"
  ogType="article"
  author="Martin Wong"
  datePublished="2026-09-09"
  dateModified="2026-09-09"
  jsonLd={[comparisonJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
>
  <div class="container section">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#55606e;margin-bottom:16px;">
      <a href="/" style="color:#1e5aa8;">Home</a> &rsaquo; Compare &rsaquo; N150 vs N200
    </nav>

    <h1 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-bottom:12px;">JIS N150 vs N200 Heavy-Duty Truck Battery</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The N200 (190H52) is larger and higher-output than the N150 (145G51) in every way — more
      capacity, more CCA, more reserve capacity and a bigger case. The real question is not which
      is "better" but which one your truck's tray and engine actually require.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:20px 0;padding:16px 20px;background:#f4f7fb;border-left:4px solid #f5a623;border-radius:6px;">
      <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin:0 0 10px;">Direct Answer</h2>
      <p style="margin:0;">
        <strong>N150 (145G51)</strong> = 12V, 135Ah, 900A CCA, 220min RC, 508×222×212mm — the standard
        heavy-truck tray size. <strong>N200 (190H52)</strong> = 12V, 200Ah, 1100A CCA, 320min RC,
        520×278×220mm — the larger, higher-output model. Both are JIS D5301 flooded batteries with
        JIS type A terminals. <strong>The deciding factor is almost always tray width</strong>: the
        N200's 278mm width will not fit an N150's 222mm tray.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Key Takeaways</h2>
    <ol class="takeaways-list">
      <li><strong>"N150" and "N200" are size classes, not capacity ratings</strong> — the model code (145G51 / 190H52) carries the actual specification.</li>
      <li><strong>The N200 is not a drop-in upgrade</strong> — it is 56mm wider (278 vs 222mm), so it usually will not fit an N150 tray.</li>
      <li><strong>1100A vs 900A CCA only matters if you need it</strong> — the N200 suits the largest engines and cold climates; the N150 covers standard heavy-truck duty.</li>
      <li><strong>Reserve capacity jumps from 220 to 320 minutes</strong> — the N200 gives more engine-off buffer for high electrical loads.</li>
      <li><strong>In a 24V truck, pair identical batteries</strong> — two N150s or two N200s, never one of each.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Specification Comparison</h2>
    <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Specification</th><th>N150 (145G51)</th><th>N200 (190H52)</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Voltage</td><td>12V</td><td>12V</td><td>Identical — both are single 12V batteries</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Capacity (C20)</td><td>135 Ah</td><td>200 Ah</td><td>N200 stores ~48% more energy</td></tr>
          <tr><td>CCA (JIS D5301)</td><td>900 A</td><td>1100 A</td><td>N200 gives a stronger cold start</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Reserve Capacity (SAE J537)</td><td>220 min</td><td>320 min</td><td>N200 = +100 min engine-off buffer</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Dimensions (L×W×H)</td><td>508×222×212 mm</td><td>520×278×220 mm</td><td>N200 is wider and taller — check the tray</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Terminal</td><td>JIS type A</td><td>JIS type A</td><td>Same terminal type</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Technology</td><td>Pb-Sb thick plate</td><td>Pb-Sb thick plate</td><td>Same flooded construction</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Typical application</td><td>Standard heavy trucks, buses, generators</td><td>Largest trucks, semi trailers, cold climates</td><td>N200 for the heaviest duty</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="data-note">
      Figures are DINWEYS first-party specifications (JIS D5301). Third-party N150 CCA commonly ranges
      ~750–1100A and dimensions vary slightly by manufacturer — always compare within one standard and
      confirm against the specific datasheet.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">What Is the Difference?</h2>
    <p>
      The N150 and N200 are the two core JIS heavy-duty battery classes. Both are 12V flooded
      batteries built on a thick-plate lead-antimony (Pb-Sb) grid — deliberately heavier than a car
      battery so they can survive the deep cycling and vibration of a large diesel engine.
    </p>
    <p>
      The difference is physical size and electrical output. The N200 is larger in every dimension
      and delivers more capacity (200Ah vs 135Ah), more cold cranking (1100A vs 900A) and more
      reserve capacity (320min vs 220min). Think of the N150 as the standard heavy-truck battery and
      the N200 as the uprated version for the largest engines and hardest conditions.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:28px;">Why the Difference Matters</h2>
    <div class="grid grid-3" style="margin-top:12px;">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Tray Fit</h3>
        <p>The N200's 278mm width vs the N150's 222mm is the real constraint. A battery that does not fit the tray and hold-down is useless regardless of its rating.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Cold-Start Margin</h3>
        <p>In cold climates or for the largest engines, 1100A turns over what 900A cannot. The N200 is the safer choice where cranking demand is highest.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Electrical Load</h3>
        <p>The extra 100 minutes of reserve capacity matters when a truck runs heavy lights, telematics and refrigeration off the battery while the engine is off.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Are They Interchangeable?</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>No, not physically.</strong> The N200's larger case (520×278×220mm vs 508×222×212mm)
      means it will not drop into an N150 tray. The reverse is also problematic: an N150 in an N200
      tray leaves it loose and may not meet the engine's cranking requirement.
    </p>
    <p>
      What <em>is</em> interchangeable is the electrical role — both are 12V batteries with JIS type A
      terminals, so in a 12V application they do the same job at different power levels. In a 24V
      system (two batteries in series), you must use two identical units.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Buyer Decision Guide</h2>
    <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Your situation</th><th>Choose</th><th>Why</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Standard heavy truck, temperate climate, N150 tray</td><td><strong>N150 (145G51)</strong></td><td>Fits the standard tray; 900A meets normal diesel cranking</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Largest engine / semi trailer / cold climate</td><td><strong>N200 (190H52)</strong></td><td>1100A cold-start margin and 320min RC for heavy duty</td></tr>
          <tr><td>High electrical load (reefer, telematics, liftgate)</td><td><strong>N200 (190H52)</strong></td><td>Extra reserve capacity sustains loads with the engine off</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Existing N150 battery, working fine</td><td><strong>N150 (145G51)</strong></td><td>Direct replacement — no tray change needed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>24V system, replacing a pair</td><td><strong>Two identical</strong></td><td>Two N150 or two N200, never mixed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wants more power but N150 tray</td><td><strong>Confirm fit first</strong></td><td>The N200 likely will not fit — verify before ordering</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">What to Check Before Ordering</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Tray length, width and height — the N200 is 56mm wider than the N150.</li>
      <li>Terminal orientation and cable reach.</li>
      <li>Engine CCA requirement — compare within JIS D5301, not against other vendors' N150 figures.</li>
      <li>Whether the vehicle is 12V or 24V (two batteries in series).</li>
      <li>Your electrical load — reserve capacity matters for engine-off accessories.</li>
    </ul>

    <div style="margin:24px 0;padding:16px 20px;background:#fff8ec;border-left:4px solid #f5a623;border-radius:6px;">
      <p style="margin:0;">
        <strong>Verification note:</strong> Specifications vary by manufacturer. The N150 and N200
        figures above are DINWEYS first-party specifications. Always confirm the exact group size,
        terminal type, polarity and tray dimensions against your vehicle manual before replacement.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is the difference between JIS N150 and N200?</h3>
      <p>The N200 (190H52) is larger and higher-output than the N150 (145G51): 200Ah vs 135Ah, 1100A vs 900A CCA, 320min vs 220min RC, and a bigger 520×278×220mm case.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Can I replace an N150 with an N200?</h3>
      <p>Usually not — the N200 is 56mm wider and will not fit an N150 tray. Only upgrade if your tray, cables and hold-down accept the larger footprint.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Which has higher CCA, N150 or N200?</h3>
      <p>The N200: 1100A vs 900A, both measured to JIS D5301. Higher CCA matters for the largest engines and cold climates.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Is N200 always the better battery?</h3>
      <p>No. It is more powerful but larger, heavier and more expensive. If the N150 meets your engine's CCA requirement and fits your tray, it is the correct and more economical choice.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What should I use in a 24V truck?</h3>
      <p>Two identical batteries in series — two N150 or two N200, never one of each — so charging stays balanced.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:32px;">
      <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;">Related Models</h2>
      <ul>
